#
# The whole notebook tour (01_ ... 09_) as one notebook: the cells of the nine files in order.
# Open it in the notebook (Open notebook...) or run it with hacs --script tour_all.oc.

# %% [md] 01 -- the space and the coefficients.
# SL_2(Z) acts on the upper half plane; the console's model is a finite triangulation of the quotient
# with its isotropy (sl2z_model()). A coefficient system is the representation Sym^n of the standard
# representation over F_ell, here n = 10 and ell = 691, so d_V = n + 1 = 11.
# In the notebook every display(...) is rendered (TeX, tables); in a script it prints the canonical text.

# %% The model of SL_2(Z)\H
# The coefficient ring is the default F_ell, stated here so that the file runs in any order in one session (08 sets Z).
set coefficient_ring = "F_ell";
M = sl2z_model();
display(M);

# %% The coefficients Sym^10 over F_691
L = symmetric_power(M, 10, 691);
display(L);

# %% [md] 02 -- cohomology, and what "declared" means.
# cohomology(L) returns a HANDLE: nothing is computed until a value is needed. In the notebook a bare
# `H` shows the handle as "declared" with a compute button; display(H) forces it first.
# Eichler--Shimura: for ell > n + 1, dim H^1(SL_2(Z); Sym^n F_ell^2) = 2 dim S_{n+2} + 1 (n even, n > 0),
# and H^0 = H^2 = 0. At n = 10 (weight 12) S_12 is spanned by Delta, so dim H^1 = 3.

# %% Setting and model
# The coefficient ring is the default F_ell, stated here so that the file runs in any order in one session (08 sets Z).
set coefficient_ring = "F_ell";
M = sl2z_model();

# %% A handle: declared, not computed
H = cohomology(symmetric_power(M, 10, 691));
H

# %% display forces the handle
display(H);

# %% A small prime sees more
# A small prime sees more: at ell = 5 and n = 20 (a Dickson degree, 20 = 5 * 4) H^0 is non-zero: dims [1, 4, 0].
display(cohomology(symmetric_power(M, 20, 5)));

# %% [md] 03 -- Hecke operators as matrices.
# hecke(H, p) is the operator T_p on H^*; T.A(q) is its matrix on H^q in the stored basis, and charpoly
# its characteristic polynomial over F_ell. On H^1(SL_2(Z); Sym^10 F_691^2) the eigenvalues of T_2 are
# tau(2) = -24 on the two-dimensional cusp part (Delta, both signs) and 1 + 2^11 on the Eisenstein line -- and mod
# 691 these agree: -24 = 2049 (mod 691), Ramanujan's congruence.

# %% Setting, model and cohomology
# The coefficient ring is the default F_ell, stated here so that the file runs in any order in one session (08 sets Z).
set coefficient_ring = "F_ell";
M = sl2z_model();
H = cohomology(symmetric_power(M, 10, 691));

# %% T_2 on H^1 and its characteristic polynomial
T2 = hecke(H, 2);
display(T2.A(1));
display(charpoly(T2.A(1)));

# %% The operator handle
# The operator handle itself: its matrices per degree and their characteristic polynomials.
display(T2);

# %% [md] 04 -- maximal ideals of the Hecke algebra and their characters.
# ext_record(n, ell) decomposes H^* under the Hecke algebra T. Each maximal ideal m has a character
# T_p -> T/m = F_ell[w]/(pi_w) = F_{ell^f}; m.character(p) is the value at T_p, written in the witness w.
# At (10, 691) there is ONE ideal: the cusp form Delta and the Eisenstein series E_12 share the
# character T_p -> 1 + p^11 (mod 691), and the local algebra is F_691[eps]/eps^2 (Hilbert function (1, 1)).

# %% The maximal ideal of ext_record(10, 691)
# The coefficient ring is the default F_ell, stated here so that the file runs in any order in one session (08 sets Z).
set coefficient_ring = "F_ell";
R = ext_record(10, 691);
m = R.ideal(0);
display(m);

# %% Its character
display(m.character(2), m.character(3));

# %% The local algebra
display(local_algebra(m));

# %% [md] 05 -- how H_m sits over T_m: Loewy layers, socle layers, Jordan types.
# module_structure(m) is the LoewyProfile of the block H^1_m over the local algebra T_m. Its Loewy layers
# (the radical filtration) and socle layers are dimensions in units of f; jordan(p) is the Jordan type of
# T_p - a_p on the block. At (10, 691) the block is 3-dimensional with Loewy layers 2 | 1 and Jordan type
# (2, 1): T_p - (1 + p^11) is nilpotent but not zero -- a non-split extension of Eisenstein by cusp.

# %% Setting and the maximal ideal
# The coefficient ring is the default F_ell, stated here so that the file runs in any order in one session (08 sets Z).
set coefficient_ring = "F_ell";
R = ext_record(10, 691);
m = R.ideal(0);

# %% The Loewy profile
P = module_structure(m);
display(P);

# %% The Jordan type of T_2
display(P.jordan(2));

# %% [md] 06 -- the Dickson long exact sequence.
# Over F_ell (ell >= 5) multiplication by the Dickson invariant gives a short exact sequence
#   0 -> Sym^n (x) det -> Sym^{n+ell+1} -> Q_n -> 0,
# hence a long exact sequence in cohomology
#   0 -> H^0(Sym^n (x) det) -> H^0(Sym^{n'}) -> H^0(Q_n) -> H^1(Sym^n (x) det) -> H^1(Sym^{n'}) -> H^1(Q_n) -> 0,
# n' = n + ell + 1. dickson_les(n, ell) computes the six terms, the five maps theta^0, pi^0, delta,
# theta^1, pi^1 (each rendered with its source and target) and certifies exactness at every spot.

# %% The sequence and its five maps
# The coefficient ring is the default F_ell, stated here so that the file runs in any order in one session (08 sets Z).
set coefficient_ring = "F_ell";
L = dickson_les(16, 7);
display(L);

# %% Dimensions and exactness
display(L.dims, L.exact);

# %% [md] 07 -- from F_ell to Z/ell^K: the Hecke algebra over a ring.
# hecke_ring_algebra(n, ell, kmax) runs the module route over Z/ell^K and reads, per block (one system of
# Hecke eigenvalues mod ell), the lattice Lambda_m = Z_ell^f (+) (+)_i Z/ell^{e_i} (free_rank f, torsion
# rendered as a sum of cyclic groups), the Hilbert functions of A_1 = T_m / ell and of A_K, and whether
# the algebra is stable in K. At (48, 7) one block carries 7-torsion.

# %% The module route over Z/7^2
# The coefficient ring is the default F_ell, stated here so that the file runs in any order in one session (08 sets Z).
set coefficient_ring = "F_ell";
R = hecke_ring_algebra(48, 7, 2);

# %% Blocks, lattices, Hilbert functions
display(R);

# %% [md] 08 -- over Z: the integral cohomology as abelian groups.
# With coefficient_ring = "Z" the descent computes H^q(Gamma_0(N); Sym^n Z^2) as abelian groups
# Z^r (+) (+) Z/p^k, and char_Z(T_p), the characteristic polynomial of T_p on the free part, over Z.
# At level 1 and n = 10: H^1 = Z^3 (+) Z/2 (+) Z/4 (+) Z/3 -- the torsion primes 2 and 3 are the small
# primes. charpoly_factored shows char_Z(T_p) factored over Z (exact, the product checked against char_Z):
# char_Z(T_2) = (X + 24)^2 (X - 2049), the roots tau(2) = -24 (twice: Delta, both signs) and 1 + 2^11 (Eisenstein).

# %% Descent at level 1, n = 10
set coefficient_ring = "Z";
D = descent(1, 10);
display(D);

# %% The integral groups
display(D.groups_inv);

# %% char_Z(T_p), factored over Z
display(D.charpoly_factored);

# %% [md] 09 -- over Z: the Hecke operators as integer matrices, torsion included.
# H^1(Gamma_0(N); Sym^n Z^2) = T (+) Z^r: T the torsion, Z^r the free part. In generators "torsion first, then a basis
# of the free part" every T_p is a block matrix [[tau, M], [0, F]]: tau acts on T, F on Z^r, and M records how the free
# generators are moved into the torsion. module_Z() computes all three. F is exact over Z, in one picked basis B
# (rule iso_dixon/v3) that is certified to be a Z-basis by one determinant, det(Phi_cert^T B) = +-1; its integers
# are printed in full (strings). The free generators are the classes [b_j] of the cocycles of B themselves, so
# T_p b_j = sum_i F_ij b_i + sum_a M_aj g_a holds as printed (g_a the torsion generators, row a of M mod d_a).
# pi_B (in retractions, per torsion prime) gives the torsion coordinates of the b_j: subtracting them gives the
# other natural complement, and M changes by pi_B F - tau pi_B. At level 1 and n = 30 the torsion is
# (Z/2)^3 + (Z/4)^3 + (Z/3)^2 + Z/9 + Z/25 + Z/29, r = 5, and the entries of F(T_3) have up to 89 bits.
# module_Z("local") is the same object without F.

# %% H^1 over Z at level 1, n = 30
set coefficient_ring = "Z";
D = descent(1, 30, [2, 3]);
Z = D.module_Z();
display(Z.invariant_factors);
display(Z.basis);

# %% T_3: the free block F over Z and its characteristic polynomial
display(Z.ops[1].F);
display(Z.ops[1].charpoly_Z);

# %% T_3: the torsion block tau and the mixed block M (per torsion prime, against the classes of B)
display(Z.ops[1].tau);
display(Z.ops[1].M);

# %% The torsion coordinates of the basis B at ell = 5 (pi_B: one row per generator of the 5-torsion)
display(Z.retractions[2].ell);
display(Z.retractions[2].pi_B);

# %% [md] 10 -- over Z: congruences and the torsion-by-free extension, as certified statements.
# Tour 09 gave the Hecke operators over Z as matrices. hecke_structure(N, n) ([C] sec:recordschema, item 8) says what they do to
# the Hecke algebra T over Z: T^fr is its image on L = H^1/torsion, and the record gives the congruences between the components of
# T^fr and how the torsion sits against L. Every field is a certified value or a named status, and the record as a whole is
# final or pending. The four records and the descent below take about two seconds and 0.25 GiB together.
#
# Level 1, n = 10 (weight 12). L has two components: Eisenstein, T_p -> 1 + p^11, and cuspidal, T_p -> tau(p) (T_2 gives 2049 and -24).
# C_T is the algebra congruence module T^fr/(I_Eis + I_cusp) ([C] def:heckeZcong). The record prints certified:0x2b3, and 0x2b3 = 691.
# It is cyclic, and T^fr = Z x_{Z/691} Z is the ring of pairs (a, b) with a = b mod 691 ([C] prop:heckeZcong(3), (4); fp=certified). The
# pair of T_n is (sigma_11(n), tau(n)), so this one ring statement is Ramanujan's tau(n) = sigma_11(n) mod 691 (for T_2: 2049 + 24 = 3 * 691).
# G2 at 691: one maximal ideal, residue X + 24 (polynomials low to high), through both components; its local ring is monogenic of
# rank 2, embedding dimension 1, Gorenstein, Hilbert function 1,1, Loewy length 2. G3's second route, v_691(B_12/12) = 1, agrees.
#
# The torsion is Z/2 (+) Z/4 (+) Z/3 (tour 08). G5: at 2 and at 3 the torsion point is common_certified with a free system (route
# T_ell_level1, [C] thm:heckeZdescent(5)(i)). G6 says split_on_P at both: the extension splits for T' = Z[computed T_p], here
# T_2, T_3, T_5. split_on_P is a statement about T' only: split for all of T would need [C] rmk:heckeZcbthree, a conjecture.

# %% Ramanujan over Z: the record of (1, 10)
set coefficient_ring = "Z";
R = hecke_structure(1, 10);
display(R.status);
display(R.C_T);
display(R.signature);

# %% The local type at 691, and the congruence module (G2 and G3 of the body)
assert(R.body[118] == "G2.ell=691.m[0].residue_degree=1", "the body of (1,10) moved");
for i in range(118, 151) { display(R.body[i]); }

# %% The torsion at 2 and 3: glue bounds, relation to the free part, split verdict (G4, G5, G6 of the body)
for i in range(151, 176) { display(R.body[i]); }

# %% [md] Torsion-by-free extensions: where the torsion does not split off.
# Fix ell; tors_ell is the ell-torsion of H^1, e_ell its exponent. For K >= e_ell let eps_K be the class of the Hecke-module sequence
# 0 -> tors_ell -> H^1_ell/ell^K -> L_ell/ell^K -> 0; the class of H^1_ell itself splits iff eps_K does ([C] prop:heckeZglue(2)(a)).
# In a basis adapted to the torsion, T_p = [[B_p, 0], [beta_p, c_p]] with B_p on L/ell^K and c_p on tors_ell; a complement of the torsion
# is the graph of an h in Hom(L/ell^e_ell, tors_ell), and it is stable under the computed T_p iff h B_p - c_p h = beta_p for all of them.
# Unsolvable for the computed subalgebra T' implies non-split for T ([C] prop:heckeZglue(2)(b)): a certificate.
# So non_split at a torsion point means exactly this: at that point the system has no solution, and hence no complement of the torsion
# is stable under T. It also certifies that this torsion system equals a free one for all p (prop:heckeZglue(4), thm:heckeZdescent(5)(ii);
# in G5: common_certified, route nonsplit). It does not give a non-zero element of the glue J: split implies J_ell = 0 and the converse
# fails (prop:heckeZglue(2)(c)), and G4 stays at bounds.
#
# These three records have overall status final (binary cf93a542, which has U_2 and U_3 over Z_(2) and Z_(3), plan card Z2). They are
# non-reduced (redQ=non_reduced_certified, C1=refused(non_reduced)); generation at 2 and 3 reads open(sandwich), and C_T reads not_run.
# (The card HZ-EISUP, the Eisenstein U_p systems, is for the records with (C1) certified, which these are not.)
# The non_split verdict does not wait: the certificate uses only T_p with p not dividing N (T_3, T_5, T_7 at N = 16), U_p is not among
# them, and [C] sec:recordschema, item 8, states that non_split and split_by_support are statements about T. split_on_P is not: at
# (27, 2), ell = 2, and (8, 4), ell = 3, it stays a statement about T'.

# %% Level 16, n = 2: non_split at 2
R = hecke_structure(16, 2);
display(R.status);
display(R.C_T);
display(R.signature);
display(R.body);

# %% Level 27, n = 2: non_split at 3, split_on_P at 2
R = hecke_structure(27, 2);
display(R.status);
display(R.C_T);
display(R.signature);

# %% Level 8, n = 4: non_split at 2, split_on_P at 3
R = hecke_structure(8, 4);
display(R.status);
display(R.C_T);
display(R.signature);

# %% [md] Against tour 09: the block M is not the verdict.
# module_Z() of tour 09 prints T_p = [[tau, M], [0, F]] on H^1 = T (+) Z^r. M is the Hom(free, torsion) block, and it depends on the chosen
# basis: moving to another complement changes M by pi_B F - tau pi_B (tour 09), the same shape as h B_p - c_p h above. So M != 0 proves
# nothing, and module_Z() does not decide: its field split is "unknown". The HeckeZ record is what decides. At (1, 30) M of T_2 is
# non-zero at 2, 3 and 29, and yet the record reads G6=[2.pt[0]=split_on_P;3.pt[0]=split_on_P;5.pt[0]=split_on_P;29.pt[0]=split_by_support].
#
# Two limits, plainly. No level-1 non_split occurs in HeckeZ edition 1: its 12 non_split lines are (8,4), (8,10), (16,2), (16,4), (27,2),
# (27,4), each full and anemic. And a basis-free class in Ext^1 is not computed: the record stores the verdict, not the class
# ([C] thm:descentcert(7)(a) leaves the extension class open).

# %% module_Z at (1, 30): M of T_2 is printed, split is unknown
D = descent(1, 30, [2, 3]);
Z = D.module_Z();
display(Z.split);
display(Z.ops[0].M);

# %% The record of the same cell decides
R = hecke_structure(1, 30);
display(R.status);
display(R.signature);
